  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests a potential relationship between Dl-Isoindolmycin and HepG2 through a series of molecular interactions. Dl-Isoindolmycin is implicated in targeting SNU-387, which in turn is a target of multiple compounds, including S-ethylcysteine zwitterion and Npc13565. Both of these compounds have been shown to interact with HepG2, indicating a possible pathway through which Dl-Isoindolmycin may exert its effects on the cell line. The scores from the different paths suggest a moderate level of confidence in this relationship, with varying degrees of support from different molecular targets. The evidence highlights the involvement of SNU-387 as a central node in the interaction network, potentially mediating the effect of Dl-Isoindolmycin on HepG2. However, the exact mechanism by which Dl-Isoindolmycin influences HepG2 remains unclear, and further experimental validation would be necessary to confirm the proposed relationship."
